    "tier": 4,
    "order_in_tier": 1,
    "title": "Sonatina in C Major (Op. 36, No. 1 - Mvt. 1 Exposition)",
    "composer_source": "Study after Muzio Clementi, Op. 36 No. 1",
    "key": {
      "fifths": 0,
      "mode": "major"
    },
    "time_signature": {
      "numerator": 4,
      "denominator": 4
    },
    "target_bpm": 108.0,
    "theory": {
      "summary": "Classic Sonatina exposition teaching tonic-to-dominant modulation (C Major -> D7 -> G Major) over crisp accompaniment.",
      "pattern_tip": "Notice the structural turning point at Bar 9 where F# arrives (D7 = V7/V), shifting the tonal center to G Major.",
      "technique_tip": "Articulate the arpeggio leaps cleanly and keep the Alberti bass rhythmic and feather-light.",
      "ballade_link": "Internalizes Classical sonata-form key architecture before tackling Chopin's extended modulatory forms.",
      "concept_title": "Secondary Dominants (V7/V) & Sonata-Exposition Modulation",
      "concept_paragraphs": [
        "In Classical Sonata Form, an Exposition always does two jobs: Theme 1 establishes the Home Key (C Major), and then a harmonic bridge pivots cleanly into the Dominant Key (G Major) for Theme 2\u2014creating long-range tonal drama.",
        "Watch Bar 10 like a hawk! When D7 (V7/V) introduces F#, it acts like a magnetic lens zooming into G Major. Note also IV/V in Bar 12: C Major suddenly sounds like the relaxed IV chord inside the new key of G!"
      ],
      "form_map": [
        {
          "phrase_label": "Phrase A (Bars 1-4)",
          "role": "Primary Theme A in C Major: bold arpeggio statement settling on Tonic C."
        },
        {
          "phrase_label": "Phrase B (Bars 5-8)",
          "role": "Scalar continuation cadencing through Dm/F (ii6) -> G7 -> C."
        },
        {
          "phrase_label": "Phrase C (Bars 9-12)",
          "role": "Modulating Bridge: F# enters via D7 (V7/V), shifting center of gravity to G Major."
        },
        {
          "phrase_label": "Phrase D (Bars 13-16)",
          "role": "Secondary Theme & closing confirmation firmly established in Dominant G."
        }
      ],
      "practice_steps": [
        "1. Notice how Bars 1-8 stay purely white-key C Major, then Bars 9-16 turn into G Major (with F#).",
        "2. Keep LH Alberti accompaniment snappy and transparent beneath RH arpeggios.",
        "3. Isolate Phrase C (Bars 9-12) first so the D7 -> G pivot feels automatic.",
        "4. Build crisp Classical articulation smoothly up to 108 BPM."
      ],
      "audio_examples": [
        {
          "label": "Before vs. After Modulation (C Home -> V7/V Pivot -> G Key)",
          "caption": "C -> F -> G7 -> C | D7 (V7/V) -> G -> C (IV/V) -> G",
          "chords": [
            "C",
            "G7",
            "C",
            "D7",
            "G"
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "How D7 (F#) Pulls Ear Away from C",
          "caption": "Compare C->G (open) with C->D7->G (resolved in G)",
          "chords": [
            "C",
            "D7",
            "G",
            "D7",
            "G"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  },
